{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You are indeed my bone and my flesh.</b> Since it says, ״וַיְסַפֵּר לְלָבָן אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״, and Scripture did not specify which things these things were that he told him — and it should have said ״אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר קָרָהוּ״ — surely then he told him those things that Esau spoke in his heart, when he said, ״יִקְרְבוּ יְמֵי אֵבֶל אָבִי וְאַהַרְגָה אֶת יַעֲקֹב אָחִי״ (Genesis 27:41). And he needed to tell him for what reason he wanted to kill him, namely on account of the birthright and the blessings. Therefore Laban said to him, ״אַךְ עַצְמִי וּבְשָׂרִי אָתָּה״ — with the word ״אַךְ״ he diminished Esau, for ״אוֹהֲבֵי עָשִׁיר רַבִּים״ and ״כָל אֲחֵי רָשׁ שְׂנֵאוּהוּ״ (Proverbs 19:4, 7). And he said accordingly: you are rich, and if so, you alone are my bone and my flesh, not Esau — and therefore you need not fear him, for he is not my kinsman." "Another interpretation: since he saw nothing with him, for he came with empty hands — if so, there is in you no kinship such as the rich bring near, but only the kinship of flesh and blood. Another interpretation: he told him how he deceived Esau in taking the birthright, so he said, ״אַךְ עַצְמִי וּבְשָׂרִי אַתָּה״ (Genesis 29:14) — a deceiver like me; therefore it is fitting to bring you near to me, for like me, like you."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:08.761086963Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}